{
  "image_name": "2a2dffb05eb86dc40567278572047aaa.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sister-chromatid cohesion is established during DNA replication and how this relates to cohesin regulation. The main idea is that cohesin rings can capture replicated DNA either by a “conversion” route, where a cohesin already associated with one DNA duplex is remodeled as the replication fork passes, or by a “de novo” route, where new cohesin is loaded behind or near the fork. In both cases, the replication machinery, including PCNA and the MCM helicase, is shown near the fork, and specific factor sets are highlighted for each pathway. The conversion pathway appears to involve fork-associated replisome proteins, whereas the de novo pathway depends on loading factors such as NIPBL and PCNA-linked components.\n\nA separate structural cartoon emphasizes regulation of the cohesin complex itself, showing the SMC3-containing ring and the PDS5 subunit. ESCO-mediated acetylation and sororin are depicted as stabilizing cohesion, while WAPL is shown as an opposing factor that promotes release.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure connects replication to chromosome organization more broadly: a moving replication fork seems to interfere with or pause cohesin-driven loop extrusion, suggesting that DNA replication both establishes cohesion and transiently alters genome-looping dynamics.",
